# Defender EASM API Runbook

Use this runbook for Microsoft Defender External Attack Surface Management
(Defender EASM) work. Resolve the target subscription, resource group, and
workspace from the active Azure CLI context; never hard-code customer values.

## Operating rules

- Start read-only. Creating, replacing, running, or deleting a discovery group
  changes the customer's inventory or consumes discovery capacity and needs an
  explicit request.
- Prefer `az rest` for both ARM and EASM data-plane calls.
- Record the method, complete path, API version, and concise response evidence.
- Do not print tokens, complete raw asset exports, or sensitive observations.
- Treat `2024-10-01-preview` as preview behavior. Verify it remains current
  before using it in automation.

## Context and endpoint discovery

```sh
az account show -o json
az rest --method get \
  --url "https://management.azure.com/subscriptions/<subscription-id>/providers/Microsoft.Easm?api-version=2021-04-01" -o json
```

The provider response is authoritative for ARM API versions. At the time this
runbook was written, the newest registered ARM workspace version was
`2023-04-01-preview`; do not assume that an API version listed in public specs
is enabled in the target subscription.

Resolve the workspace and use its `properties.dataPlaneEndpoint`:

```sh
az rest --method get --url \
  "https://management.azure.com/subscriptions/<subscription-id>/resourceGroups/<resource-group>/providers/Microsoft.Easm/workspaces/<workspace>?api-version=2023-04-01-preview" \
  -o json
```

Build the data-plane base URL as:

```text
https://<dataPlaneEndpoint>/subscriptions/<subscription-id>/resourceGroups/<resource-group>/workspaces/<workspace>
```

Every data-plane request needs the EASM resource audience:

```sh
az rest --method get --resource 'https://easm.defender.microsoft.com' --url \
  "<base-url>/assets?api-version=2024-10-01-preview&maxpagesize=100&responseType=REDUCED"
```

## API surface

Control plane (ARM): workspaces, workspace labels, and workspace tasks.

Data plane (`2024-10-01-preview`): assets (including export, deltas, and
observations), discovery groups and runs, discovery templates, reports, saved
filters, policies, data connections, and tasks. Use the current Microsoft Learn
REST reference to confirm an operation's verb and payload before a mutation.

Useful read operations:

```text
GET  <base-url>/assets?api-version=2024-10-01-preview
GET  <base-url>/assets/{assetId}?api-version=2024-10-01-preview
POST <base-url>/assets/{assetId}:getObservations?api-version=2024-10-01-preview
GET  <base-url>/discoGroups?api-version=2024-10-01-preview
GET  <base-url>/discoGroups/{groupName}?api-version=2024-10-01-preview
GET  <base-url>/discoTemplates?api-version=2024-10-01-preview
GET  <base-url>/dataConnections?api-version=2024-10-01-preview
GET  <base-url>/savedFilters?api-version=2024-10-01-preview
GET  <base-url>/policies?api-version=2024-10-01-preview
GET  <base-url>/tasks?api-version=2024-10-01-preview
POST <base-url>/reports/assets:getSummary?api-version=2024-10-01-preview
```

Use `maxpagesize` within the endpoint limit and follow `nextLink` or `mark`.
Do not assume `skip` is sufficient for every asset query.

## Asset IDs and findings

EASM asset IDs in paths are base64-encoded. Encode the logical ID before using
it in a path and URL-encode padding:

```sh
printf '%s' 'ipAddress$$<public-ip>' | base64 -w 0
```

Then query observations with a POST (an empty JSON body is valid):

```sh
az rest --method post --resource 'https://easm.defender.microsoft.com' \
  --url "<base-url>/assets/<base64-asset-id>:getObservations?api-version=2024-10-01-preview&maxpagesize=100" \
  --body '{}'
```

Use `prioritySummary` and `value` as the evidence for High/Medium/Low findings.
If `totalElements` is zero, state that there are no EASM observations rather
than converting asset metadata or old scan banners into findings.

## Add a domain or public IP CIDR

Domains and public CIDRs are discovery *seeds*, not assets to POST directly.
Create or replace a discovery group with `PUT /discoGroups/{groupName}`. Use
`kind: domain` for a domain and `kind: ipBlock` for a CIDR. Private addresses
cannot be discovery seeds; CIDRs must use their network address.

Example body (replace placeholders; do not reuse the existing group's whole
body blindly):

```json
{
  "description": "Managed discovery seeds",
  "frequencyMilliseconds": 604800000,
  "tier": "advanced",
  "seeds": [
    { "name": "example.com", "kind": "domain" },
    { "name": "203.0.113.0/24", "kind": "ipBlock" }
  ],
  "excludes": []
}
```

```sh
az rest --method put --resource 'https://easm.defender.microsoft.com' \
  --url "<base-url>/discoGroups/<url-encoded-group-name>?api-version=2024-10-01-preview" \
  --body '<validated-json>'
```

`PUT` replaces the group configuration. First `GET` the group, merge the new
seed without removing existing seeds/exclusions, show the proposed diff, then
write. To start discovery after approval:

```sh
az rest --method post --resource 'https://easm.defender.microsoft.com' \
  --url "<base-url>/discoGroups/<url-encoded-group-name>:run?api-version=2024-10-01-preview" \
  --body '{}'
```

Poll the group or `/tasks` until the run reaches a terminal state.

## Validation checklist

1. Confirm active account and subscription.
2. Read the Microsoft.Easm provider and target workspace.
3. Confirm data-plane endpoint and use the EASM token audience.
4. List discovery groups before and after any approved change.
5. Re-read the changed group, verify seeds/exclusions/frequency, then run only
   when requested.
6. Query observations by encoded asset ID for finding counts and priorities.
